LATTICE THEORY BASICS
  Poset (P, ≤)   (non-strict dual of the tree above)

  Meet a ∧ b     = greatest lower bound (inf)
  Join a ∨ b     = least upper bound (sup)

  Semilattice
    meet-semilattice: every pair has a ∧ b
    join-semilattice: every pair has a ∨ b

  Lattice
    every pair has both meet and join
    (equivalently: algebraic structure with ∧, ∨
     that are commutative, associative, idempotent
     and satisfy absorption: a ∨ (a ∧ b) = a)

  Bounded lattice     has 0 (bottom) and 1 (top)
  Complete lattice    every subset (not just pairs) has inf and sup
  Distributive        a ∧ (b ∨ c) = (a ∧ b) ∨ (a ∧ c)
  Modular             a ≤ c  ⇒  a ∨ (b ∧ c) = (a ∨ b) ∧ c
  Boolean algebra     bounded distributive complemented lattice
  Heyting algebra     bounded lattice with relative pseudocomplement
                      (intuitionistic logic)

  Examples
    power set (⊆, ∩, ∪)          complete Boolean
    subspaces of a vector space  modular, not always distributive
    open sets of a space         complete Heyting (frame)

